        title: "The Umwandlungsgesetz: Formwechsel, Verschmelzung, Spaltung",
        explanation:
          "The Umwandlungsgesetz (UmwG) provides three distinct structural transactions, each with its own formal procedure and legal consequence. A Formwechsel, change of legal form, under Sections 190 and following UmwG, converts an entity from one recognized type to another, an OHG becoming a GmbH, for instance, while the converting entity is expressly deemed under Section 202 UmwG to be the same legal person throughout, its existing contracts, permits, and registered assets simply continue under the new form with no individual reassignment required. Verschmelzung, merger, under Sections 2 and following UmwG, transfers one entity's entire assets and liabilities to another by Gesamtrechtsnachfolge, universal succession, under Section 20 UmwG, the moment the merger is entered in the Handelsregister, with the absorbed entity's separate existence then simply ending; Spaltung, division, under Sections 123 and following UmwG, comes in three distinct forms, Aufspaltung, splitting one entity entirely into two or more new entities, Abspaltung, carving part of an entity's business out into a separate entity while the original entity continues to exist, and Ausgliederung, transferring part of a business into a new subsidiary in exchange for shares in that subsidiary. Section 22 UmwG gives a creditor of any participating entity, in a merger or a division, a right to demand security (Sicherheitsleistung) for a claim that arose before the transaction's publication and is not yet due, if that creditor can show the transaction genuinely endangers satisfaction of the claim, and Section 133 UmwG specifically addresses division, making every resulting entity jointly and severally liable, for a period of years, for liabilities allocated to any other resulting entity by the division plan, directly protecting a creditor whose specific debt the division plan assigned to the weaker resulting entity.",
        whyItMatters:
          "You should treat Gesamtrechtsnachfolge as doing exactly the same structural work as the common-law universal-succession doctrine covered in the US entry, assets and liabilities transfer automatically, together, without needing to individually assign each contract, but recognize that the UmwG's Section 133 joint-liability rule for divisions is a specifically codified creditor protection with no precise common-law equivalent, directly answering the question of what happens when a division plan allocates a specific debt to the weaker of two resulting entities. You should also never advise a client that a Formwechsel means winding up the old entity and starting fresh; Section 202 UmwG's continuity-of-identity rule is the entire point of using a Formwechsel rather than simply dissolving the old entity and forming a new one from scratch.",
        example:
          "A GbR that undergoes a Formwechsel into a GmbH keeps its existing supplier contracts and office lease in force automatically, with no individual counterparty consent required, since Section 202 UmwG treats the GmbH as the very same legal person the GbR always was, just now in a different legal form. A creditor of a company carved apart through an Abspaltung, whose specific debt the division plan assigns to the less well-capitalized of the two resulting entities, retains a claim against the other, better-capitalized resulting entity too for several years under Section 133 UmwG's joint-liability rule, regardless of what the division plan's own internal allocation says.",
